  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday morning through next Saturday)\nIssued at 233 PM EDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nRain is expected to come to an end overnight Tuesday morning. Dry \nair will settle in across much of the area north of a line from \nColumbus to Augusta with high temperatures in the mid to upper 70s. \nOvernight lows will fall into the 50s with 40's likely in the higher \nelevations of northern Georgia. By the time we get to Thursday, rain \nchances for the CWA drop to nearly zero. \n\nCurrently the NHC is keeping an eye on a disturbance off the Yucatan \nthat they're giving a 30% chance of development for. As of writing \nthis, all of the major models are developing it into some kind of \ntropical system with landfall expected somewhere along the Gulf \ncoast east of NOLA. This would bring another round of wet weather to \nthe area by next weekend, but the extent and intensity of this \nsystem is currently only speculation. For now we've kept likely \nPoPs in the grid for Saturday. \n\nPlease continue to monitor the forecast as more information becomes \navailable in regards to the developing tropics. For the most up to \ndate information, please see the National Hurricane Center's \ndiscussion and forecast.",
